/-! # Smooth resolution of a quasi-split marked nodal tower

Source: Illusie--Laszlo--Orgogozo, Travaux de Gabber, X Lemma 2.2 proof,
printed pp. 149--150 (blueprint: lem-sfb-quasi-split-tower-smooth-resolution).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u v

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits TopologicalSpace

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- Compatible equivariant smooth projective resolutions of the marked tower. The initial
identification is fixed and the zeroth modification is its identity. `C i` lists the reduced
irreducible boundary components; their stalk ideals are generated by distinct parameters.
The last clause uses the actual radical of the Cartier pullback and selects components
from this same list, including the empty selection.

Source: ILO, Travaux de Gabber, X Lemma 2.2 proof, printed pp. 149--150.
(blueprint: lem-sfb-quasi-split-tower-smooth-resolution).

Proof (the nine steps of the node):
1. First each $T_i$ is H-projective over $F$. Indeed $T_0=\operatorname{Spec}F=\mathbf P^0_F$ has
   an ample invertible sheaf. Inductively the projective-bundle morphism $f_i$ is H-projective
   because its target has an ample invertible sheaf; H-projective composition gives an
   H-projective embedding of $T_i$ over $F$, whose pulled-back twisting sheaf is ample. Thus
   every $T_i$ has an ample invertible sheaf. We now induct on $i$, constructing the asserted
   $S_i,r_i$, additionally keeping $r_i$ an isomorphism over $T_i\setminus Z_i$. Start with
   $S_0=T_0$, $r_0=\mathrm{id}$ and $E_0=\varnothing$. The spectrum of a field is regular, smooth
   and H-projective over itself, and the empty divisor satisfies all boundary conditions.
   Uses: lem-trf-projective-bundle-to-h-projective, lem-trf-h-projective-composition, lem-weil-
   pbc-projective-ample-invertible.
2. Suppose $S_{i-1},r_{i-1}$ are constructed. Form $W=T_i\times_{T_{i-1}}S_{i-1}$ with
   projections $g:W\to S_{i-1}$ and $q:W\to T_i$, and pull back the markings to $N$. Apply nodal
   birational base-change geometry with $S=T_{i-1}$, $D=Z_{i-1}$ and $t=r_{i-1}$. It gives
   integral Noetherian finite-type $W$, projective proper $g,q$, birational surjective $q$, the
   nodal, flatness, finite-presentation and generic-fibre properties for $g$, and disjoint smooth
   sections $N$. Write $E=E_{i-1}$ and $B=|N|\cup g^{-1}|E|$. Since $|E|=r_{i-1}^{-1}Z_{i-1}$,
   $B=q^{-1}Z_i$. The diagonal semilinear action preserves $B$.
   Uses: lem-sfb-nodal-birational-base-change-geometry.
3. The smooth pair $(S_{i-1},E)$ and $g,N$ satisfy the marked nodal relative log-chart theorem:
   $g$ is proper, flat, finitely presented, nodal, and smooth off $|E|$, and $N$ consists of
   disjoint sections in its smooth locus. Consequently $U=W\setminus B$ is dense and the
   divisorial pair $(W,B)$ is fine saturated, log regular and log smooth over the trivial log
   field $F$. This statement requires neither stabilizer triviality nor perfection of $F$.
   Uses: lem-sfb-marked-nodal-relative-log-charts.
4. Apply equivariant log-smooth fan regularization to $(W,B)$ and its finite semilinear
   $J$-action. It produces $a:W_1\to W$, projective in the projective-bundle sense, birational,
   equivariant and isomorphic over $U$, with $W_1$ integral smooth finite type over $F$. Its
   reduced boundary $B_1=(a^{-1}B)_{\mathrm{red}}$ has, etale locally at each geometric point, a
   smooth $F$-map to $\mathbf A^r_F$ pulling back $V(z_1\cdots z_r)$, with all $r$ branches
   passing through that point. These are exactly the chart hypotheses required in Step 5. It is
   effective Cartier by the free-characteristic regular-boundary theorem, applied to the final
   log regular stage with free characteristic monoids and triviality locus $a^{-1}U$. The whole
   action lifts with its group law, including permutations of branches.
   Uses: lem-sfb-equivariant-log-smooth-fan-regularization, lem-trf-free-characteristic-regular-
   boundary.
5. Apply relative maximal-branch strictification to $(W_1,B_1)$ with its lifted action. Obtain
   $b:S_i\to W_1$, projective birational and equivariant, isomorphic off $B_1$, with $S_i$
   integral regular smooth finite type over $F$ and $E_i=(b^{-1}B_1)_{\mathrm{red}}$ relative
   strict normal crossings. The cited tower terminates with empty old strict transform and full
   boundary equal to its relative SNC accumulated boundary; a terminal one-branch Cartier blowup
   may be the identity. Its hypotheses allow self-intersections and arbitrary permutations of
   branches and impose no condition on point stabilizers. In particular they apply even if the
   original quasi-split fibres carry nontrivial graph actions.
   Uses: lem-sfb-relative-nc-maximal-branch-strictification.
6. Set $r_i=qab:S_i\to T_i$ and use $gab:S_i\to S_{i-1}$ as the tower map. Their square commutes
   by the fibre product identity. Projective-bundle composition applies over the Noetherian
   targets and makes both composites projective in that convention. Each of $a,b$ is proper,
   hence closed, and has image containing a dense open of its integral target, so it is
   surjective. Together with the base-change theorem for $q$, this gives surjectivity and
   birationality of $r_i$. For the dense-open assertion one can intersect the dense isomorphism
   opens for the three birational maps; their inverses identify the resulting nonempty opens and
   function fields. Above $T_i\setminus Z_i$, $q$ is an isomorphism because $r_{i-1}$ is one off
   $Z_{i-1}$, and $a,b$ are isomorphisms there. Reduction does not change support, hence
   $|E_i|=r_i^{-1}Z_i$. The targets $T_i,S_{i-1}$ have ample invertible sheaves by Step 1 and
   induction, so projective-bundle-to-H-projective and H-projective composition make $r_i$
   H-projective and $S_i$ H-projective over $F$. The maps are equivariant by composition. This
   completes the induction.
   Uses: lem-sfb-projective-bundle-composition, lem-trf-projective-bundle-proper, def-weil-
   proper-morphism, lem-trf-projective-bundle-to-h-projective, lem-trf-h-projective-composition,
   lem-weil-pbc-projective-ample-invertible, lem-sfb-nodal-birational-base-change-geometry.
7. Every $S_i$ and every intersection of components of $E_i$ is smooth over $F$, so all their
   local rings are regular. We check the strict normal crossings boundary definition for $E_i$:
   its distinct reduced components and all their intersections $C_K$ (indexed by sets $K$ of
   components) are regular. At $z\in C_K$ put $A=\mathcal O_{S_i,z}$ and let $I$ be the stalk of
   the ideal of $C_K$. The quotient $A/I=\mathcal O_{C_K,z}$ is regular local, hence a domain, so
   $I$ is prime; by the relative definition, computed on an affine open and localized at $z$, its
   height is $|K|$. Every localization of $A$ is a local ring of $S_i$, hence regular, so $A$ is
   catenary by the regular-ring universal catenarity theorem. In the catenary local domain $A$
   all saturated chains from $(0)$ to the maximal ideal have length $\dim A$; concatenating
   maximal-length chains $(0)\subset\cdots\subset I$ and $I\subset\cdots\subset\mathfrak m$,
   which are saturated, gives $\dim A=|K|+\dim A/I$, the codimension required by the boundary
   definition. The boundary parameter criterion yields $I_{E_i,x}=(t_1\cdots t_r)$ for distinct
   members of a regular parameter system of $A=\mathcal O_{S_i,x}$. The regular-local UFD theorem
   makes the $t_j$ prime. The minimal primes of this product are exactly $(t_j)$: a prime
   containing the product contains a factor, and distinct parameter primes are incomparable. Thus
   the components through $x$ have precisely these individual parameter equations. Off $E_i$ take
   $r=0$.
   Uses: lem-weil-pbc-smooth-scheme-over-field-regular, def-trf-smooth-strict-normal-crossings-
   divisor, def-trf-strict-normal-crossings-boundary, lem-sfb-snc-boundary-parameter-criterion,
   lem-trf-regular-local-ufd-boundary-factorization, lem-trf-regular-ring-universally-catenary.
8. For the final clause, let $a:T_d\to X$ denote the map in the statement and put $h=ar_d:S_d\to
   X$. Dominance sends the generic point of the integral source to that of the integral target
   and induces an injection of function fields. A local Cartier equation $c$ of $C$ is nonzero in
   $k(X)$; its image is nonzero in $k(S_d)$. Stalks on an integral scheme inject into its
   function field, so its pullback is a nonzero element of every source stalk where it is
   defined. Those stalks are domains, so this equation is a nonzerodivisor and $h^{-1}C$ is
   effective Cartier. Its support lies in $|E_d|$ by the given containment. At $x$, use the
   parameters of Step 7 and put $T=t_1\cdots t_r$. Its local equation $f$ is a unit in $A[1/T]$:
   its vanishing set there is empty, so it belongs to no maximal ideal. The UFD boundary
   factorization theorem gives $f=u\prod_jt_j^{a_j}$ with $a_j\geq0$. Consequently
   $\sqrt{(f)}=(\prod_{a_j>0}t_j)$, since the $t_j$ are pairwise nonassociate primes. The empty
   product is $1$.
   Uses: lem-sc-integral-scheme-stalks-in-function-field, def-nagata-effective-cartier-ideal,
   lem-trf-regular-local-ufd-boundary-factorization.
9. Globally choose exactly the components of $E_d$ whose generic points lie in $h^{-1}|C|$. The
   factorization in Step 8 shows at each stalk that the reduced inverse image is the union of
   these components: a component occurs locally exactly when its prime factor divides a Cartier
   equation, equivalently when that equation vanishes at its generic point. Thus this union
   agrees scheme-theoretically with $(h^{-1}C)_{\mathrm{red}}$, and its local product equations
   are nonzerodivisors. It is effective Cartier. Its components and their intersections are a
   subfamily of those of $E_d$, so they remain smooth over $F$ of the required codimensions. It
   is therefore relative strict normal crossings. Its union is $J$-invariant because $h,C$ are
   equivariant. Empty markings cause no change in the induction; for empty $C$ all exponents are
   zero. For $d=0$, $S_0=\operatorname{Spec}F$ and $Z_0=\varnothing$, so the stipulated
   containment forces the Cartier pullback to be empty and the construction is the identity.
   Uses: def-trf-smooth-strict-normal-crossings-divisor, def-nagata-effective-cartier-ideal.
-/
theorem exists_quasiSplitTower_smooth_resolution
    (F : Type u) [Field F] (J : Type v) [Group J] [Finite J]
    (γ : J →* (F ≃+* F)) (d : ℕ) (T : Fin (d + 1) → Scheme.{u})
    (q : ∀ i, T i ⟶ Spec (.of F)) (e₀ : T 0 ≅ Spec (.of F))
    (hq₀ : q 0 = e₀.hom) (f : ∀ i : Fin d, T i.succ ⟶ T i.castSucc)
    (τ : ∀ i, J →* Aut (T i))
    [hIntegral : ∀ i, IsIntegral (T i)]
    [hft : ∀ i, LocallyOfFiniteType (q i)] [hqc : ∀ i, QuasiCompact (q i)]
    [hfp : ∀ i, LocallyOfFinitePresentation (f i)]
    (hq : ∀ i : Fin d, f i ≫ q i.castSucc = q i.succ)
    (hsemilinear : ∀ (i : Fin (d + 1)) (g : J), (τ i g).hom ≫ q i =
      q i ≫ Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom (γ g⁻¹).toRingHom))
    (hequivariant : ∀ (i : Fin d) (g : J), (τ i.succ g).hom ≫ f i =
      f i ≫ (τ i.castSucc g).hom)
    (hprojective : ∀ i, IsProjectiveBundle (f i)) (hflat : ∀ i, Flat (f i))
    (hnodal : ∀ i, HasNodalGeometricFibres (f i))
    (hreduced : ∀ i, GeometricallyReduced (f i))
    (hgeneric : ∀ i : Fin d, Smooth ((f i).fiberToSpecResidueField
        (genericPoint (T i.castSucc))) ∧
      GeometricallyIntegral ((f i).fiberToSpecResidueField (genericPoint (T i.castSucc))))
    (hquasiSplit : ∀ i, HasQuasiSplitNodalFibres (f i))
    (m : Fin d → ℕ) (s : ∀ i : Fin d, Fin (m i) → (T i.castSucc ⟶ T i.succ))
    (hsections : ∀ (i : Fin d) (j : Fin (m i)), s i j ≫ f i = 𝟙 (T i.castSucc))
    (hdisjoint : ∀ i : Fin d, Pairwise (fun j l : Fin (m i) ↦
      Disjoint (Set.range (s i j)) (Set.range (s i l))))
    (hsmooth : ∀ (i : Fin d) (j : Fin (m i)),
      Set.range (s i j) ⊆ ((f i).smoothLocus : Set (T i.succ)))
    (hmarkings : ∀ (i : Fin d) (g : J),
      (τ i.succ g).hom '' (⋃ j : Fin (m i), Set.range (s i j)) =
        ⋃ j : Fin (m i), Set.range (s i j))
    (Z : ∀ i, Closeds (T i)) (hZ₀ : (Z 0 : Set (T 0)) = ∅)
    (hZ : ∀ i : Fin d, (Z i.succ : Set (T i.succ)) =
      (f i) ⁻¹' (Z i.castSucc : Set (T i.castSucc)) ∪
        ⋃ j : Fin (m i), Set.range (s i j))
    (hsmoothOff : ∀ i : Fin d,
      Smooth ((f i) ∣_ (⟨(Z i.castSucc : Set (T i.castSucc))ᶜ,
        (Z i.castSucc).isClosed.isOpen_compl⟩ : (T i.castSucc).Opens))) :
    ∃ (S : Fin (d + 1) → Scheme.{u}) (p : ∀ i, S i ⟶ Spec (.of F))
      (r : ∀ i, S i ⟶ T i) (ρ : ∀ i, J →* Aut (S i))
      (g : ∀ i : Fin d, S i.succ ⟶ S i.castSucc)
      (h₀ : S 0 = T 0) (_ : ∀ i, IsIntegral (S i)) (_ : ∀ i, IsRegular (S i))
      (E : ∀ i, (S i).IdealSheafData) (n : Fin (d + 1) → ℕ)
      (C : ∀ i, Fin (n i) → (S i).IdealSheafData),
      r 0 = eqToHom h₀ ∧
      (∀ i, r i ≫ q i = p i) ∧
      (∀ i, Smooth (p i) ∧ IsHProjective (p i) ∧ IsRegularScheme (S i)) ∧
      (∀ (i : Fin (d + 1)) (a : J), (ρ i a).hom ≫ p i =
        p i ≫ Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom (γ a⁻¹).toRingHom)) ∧
      (∀ (i : Fin (d + 1)) (a : J), (ρ i a).hom ≫ r i = r i ≫ (τ i a).hom) ∧
      (∀ (i : Fin d) (a : J), (ρ i.succ a).hom ≫ g i = g i ≫ (ρ i.castSucc a).hom) ∧
      (∀ i : Fin d, g i ≫ r i.castSucc = r i.succ ≫ f i) ∧
      (∀ i, IsHProjective (r i) ∧ Surjective (r i) ∧
        (∃ (U : (S i).Opens) (V : (T i).Opens) (e : U.toScheme ≅ V.toScheme),
          Dense (U : Set (S i)) ∧ Dense (V : Set (T i)) ∧
          e.hom ≫ V.ι = U.ι ≫ r i) ∧
        ∃ hη : r i (genericPoint (S i)) = genericPoint (T i),
          Function.Bijective
            (((T i).presheaf.stalkCongr (Inseparable.of_eq hη)).inv ≫
              (r i).stalkMap (genericPoint (S i)) : (T i).functionField ⟶ (S i).functionField)) ∧
      (∀ i, E i = ((Scheme.IdealSheafData.vanishingIdeal (Z i)).comap (r i)).radical ∧
        (E i).IsSmoothStrictNormalCrossingsDivisor (p i) ∧
        E i = ⨅ j, C i j ∧
        (∀ j, IsIntegral (C i j).subscheme) ∧
        (∀ j k, (C i j).support ≤ (C i k).support → j = k) ∧
        ∀ x : S i, ∃ t : List ((S i).presheaf.stalk x),
          IsLocalRing.IsRegularSystemOfParameters t ∧
          ∃ (K : Finset (Fin (n i))) (κ : K → Fin t.length),
            Function.Injective κ ∧
            (∀ j, x ∈ (C i j).support ↔ j ∈ K) ∧
            ∀ j : K, ∃ (U : (S i).affineOpens) (hx : x ∈ U.1),
              ((C i j.val).ideal U).map ((S i).presheaf.germ U.1 x hx).hom =
                Ideal.span {t.get (κ j)}) ∧
      (∀ (X : Scheme.{u}) [IsIntegral X] (α : J →* Aut X)
        (D : X.IdealSheafData) (hD : D.IsInvertible)
        (hDstable : ∀ a : J, D.comap (α a).hom = D)
        (a : T (Fin.last d) ⟶ X) [IsDominant a],
        (∀ b : J, (τ (Fin.last d) b).hom ≫ a = a ≫ (α b).hom) →
        a ⁻¹' (D.support : Set X) ⊆ (Z (Fin.last d) : Set (T (Fin.last d))) →
        (D.comap (r (Fin.last d) ≫ a)).radical.IsInvertible ∧
        (∃ K : Finset (Fin (n (Fin.last d))),
          (D.comap (r (Fin.last d) ≫ a)).radical = ⨅ j ∈ K, C (Fin.last d) j) ∧
        (D.comap (r (Fin.last d) ≫ a)).radical.IsSmoothStrictNormalCrossingsDivisor
          (p (Fin.last d))) := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
